Here's a C++ function that converts Markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <iostream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::stringstream ss(markdown);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(ss, line)) {
        html += line + "\n";
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing Markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::stringstream` to read the Markdown text and appends each line to the HTML string.

Here's an example usage:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "Hello\nThis is a test";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
<p>Hello</p>
<p>This is a test</p>
```

Note that this function assumes that the Markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any special characters. You may want to add error handling and validation to make the function more robust.

